<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Forcalquier</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Forcalquier is a hill town in the Alpes-de-Haute-Provence <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. From the 11th century to 1209 it was the capital of a county formed from the inheritance of the counts of Provence, covering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durance">Durance</a> valley and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisteron">Sisteron</a>. Its citadel hill, former cathedral and Franciscan convent survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/forcalquier.jpg" alt="Forcalquier" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Forcalquier · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Forcalquier_France_Luberon.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Forcalquier lies between the Lure Mountain and Luberon ranges, about 30 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisteron">Sisteron</a> and 10 km west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durance">Durance</a> river. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name, Furnus Calcarius, comes from the lime kilns used in Roman times, and a Roman bridge still stands in the valley to the south of the town. The town is built round the slopes of a steep conical hill.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At the end of the 11th century a family of the counts of Provence created the county of Forcalquier. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the town was the capital of Haute-Provence along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durance">Durance</a>, a territory that included Manosque, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisteron">Sisteron</a>, Gap and Embrun. It minted its own currency, and its church was raised to the status of a concathedral.</p><p>The counts of Forcalquier grew powerful enough to defy the counts of Provence. The rivalry ended in 1195, when Gersende de Sabran, countess of Forcalquier, married Alfonso II, count of Provence, and their son Ramon Bérenger IV inherited both counties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The hill is crowned by the octagonal chapel of Notre Dame de Provence, built in 1875 where the medieval citadel stood until its destruction in 1601. The oldest part of the town surrounds the Place Saint-Michel, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> fountain of 1511 and doorways from the 12th to 16th centuries. The 12th-century concathedral of Notre Dame de l'Assomption stands across from the Place du Bourguet. Franciscans, called cordeliers for their rope belts, built a convent in the 13th century, occupied by monks until the Revolution. The Port de Cordeliers is all that remains of the town walls. The municipal museum holds prehistoric and Gallo-Roman objects, glass and local pottery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Forcalquier” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The county arose from the division of the inheritance of the counts of Provence in the mid-11th century, when one branch kept rights in the north-east around <b>Forcalquier</b>, Sisteron and the Durance valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-forcalquier">County of Forcalquier</a></li></ul></section>
